WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Although we are currently in the midst of a global pandemic, we should not forget that we are fortunate to live in a time where we have vaccines and other public health measures we can take to avoid illnesses such as polio, smallpox, cholera, even bubonic plague. And we hope that soon COVID-19 will be vanquished by vaccines. But for our ancestors, infectious diseases were a constant danger, so much so that the threat of infection has actually helped shape human evolution. Disgust, wariness of strangers, cultural norms around food and cleanliness, all of these behaviors may have evolved, at least in part, to keep us safe from infectious disease. Researchers call this suite of protective mechanisms the behavioral immune system, but are the defenses that humans evolved thousands of years ago to ward off infection suitable to protect us from the diseases we face today?
尽管我们目前正处于一场全球大流行之中，但我们不应忘记，我们很幸运能生活在这样一个时代：我们拥有疫苗和其他公共卫生措施，可以用来预防脊髓灰质炎、天花、霍乱甚至鼠疫等疾病。我们希望新冠肺炎也能很快被疫苗战胜。但对我们的祖先来说，传染病是一种持续存在的危险，以至于感染的威胁实际上帮助塑造了人类的进化。厌恶感、对陌生人的警惕、围绕食物和清洁的文化规范，所有这些行为的演变，至少有一部分是为了保护我们免受传染病的侵害。研究人员将这一套保护机制称为行为免疫系统，但是人类在数千年前进化出的用于抵御感染的防御机制，是否适合保护我们免受当今面临的疾病的侵害？

2
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Do they do more harm than good in some cases? and what is the role of disgust in keeping us safe and healthy? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. Our guest today is Dr. Mark Schaller, a professor of psychology at the University of British Columbia.
在某些情况下，它们到底是利大于弊还是弊大于利？厌恶感在保持我们安全和健康方面起着什么作用？欢迎收看《心理学说话》，这是美国心理学协会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯。我们今天的嘉宾是英属哥伦比亚大学心理学教授马克·沙勒博士。

