But I actually think a less obvious answer is probably a more compelling answer to the question, which has to do with the first. fact that a lot of the things that people historically have done in order to avoid infection had to do with doing things a certain way. Prepare food a certain way. We have these norms of hygiene, and we have norms that govern what are appropriate ways of engaging in intimate, interpersonal contact with other folks. So we have these rituals and norms. And prior to contemporary technological advances in medicine and pharmacology and public health, these kind of rituals and norms were kind of most of what people had to help them figure out how to avoid coming into contact with infectious diseases.
但我实际上认为，一个不太显而易见的答案可能是一个更引人注目的答案，这与第一个事实有关，即历史上人们为了避免感染而做的很多事情都与以某种特定方式做事有关。以某种特定方式准备食物。我们有这些卫生规范，我们有规范来管理与其他人进行亲密的人际接触的适当方式。所以我们有这些仪式和规范。在当代医学、药学和公共卫生技术进步之前，这类仪式和规范可以说是人们拥有的全部，用以帮助他们弄清楚如何避免接触传染病。

